  "notes": "Standard (eastern) Nepali, the variety of the national media. Consonants: a four-way laryngeal contrast on the stops, a dental versus retroflex contrast, and AFFRICATES THAT ARE ALVEOLAR /ts tsʰ dz dzʱ/, not palato-alveolar — palatography puts the contact in the alveolar region, though the sounds strike English and French ears as palato-alveolar (Khatiwada 2009: 375, reporting Pokharel 1989 and Clements & Khatiwada 2007). ONE SIBILANT: Nepali has exactly two contrastive fricatives, /s/ and /ɦ/ (Khatiwada 2009: 376), so written श, ष and स all read /s/ — आकाश [äkäs], भाषा [bʱäsä]. NASALS: only /m n ŋ/ are phonologically pertinent; /ŋ/ is otherwise word-final only, and written ण also has a retroflex nasal flap realisation [ɽ̃] (Khatiwada 2009: 377). VOWELS: six oral qualities /i e u o ʌ ä/ with distinctive nasalisation on all but /o/, and NO LENGTH CONTRAST — the script's long/short ⟨इ ई⟩ and ⟨उ ऊ⟩ pairs are an orthographic inheritance with no counterpart in the spoken language (Khatiwada 2009: 378). The inherent vowel is /ʌ/, not the schwa [ə] of the older descriptions: it is acoustically lower and more back than a schwa (Khatiwada 2009: 377, replacing the [ə] of Bandhu et al. 1971 and Dahal 1974). The low vowel is central, written here [ä] for the same reason the dental stops are written [t̪]. SCHWA DELETION is conjunct-blocked; see NE_SCHWA_KEEP_b and NE_SCHWA_FINAL_b. व reads /w/: Nepali has no /v/ or /ʋ/ phoneme, and [w] is the nonsyllabic variant of /u/ (Khatiwada 2009: 373); in combination with /e i o ʌi̯ r w j/ it is instead the bilabial stop [b] (Wikipedia, 'Va (Indic)'), which is why [b] is listed as its allophone. The aspirated labials and velars also have homorganic fricative realisations in spontaneous speech — /sʌpʰa/ [sʌɸa], /sʌbʱa/ [sʌβa], /sakʰa/ [saxa], /ʌɡʱaɖi/ [ʌɣaɽi] (Khatiwada 2009: 376) — which is why [ɸ β x ɣ] are listed as allophones. Stress and pitch are non-distinctive (Khatiwada 2009: 378). Not modelled: intervocalic /ɦ/ deletion with compensatory lengthening, a spontaneous-speech process (Khatiwada 2009: 378); the ⟨्य⟩ conjunct's gemination-plus-[e] outcome; and gemination, which is contrastive but not predictable from the spelling of a conjunct. Only the first of Wikipedia's four final-schwa retention rules is modelled. Rules 2 and 3 — every verb form retains the schwa unless a halanta cancels it, and adverbs, onomatopoeia and postpositions usually retain it — need a part of speech the engine does not have, and rule 4 is a short list of lexical exceptions (दुःख, सुख). Rule 1 itself over-retains on the nasal conjuncts ⟨ञ्च ञ्ज⟩, whose nasal-plus-affricate coda is legal word-finally: मञ्च comes out [mʌntsʌ] where the schwa should go. The visarga ⟨ः⟩ is read silently and its own outcome is unmodelled: it neither geminates a following consonant (दुःख [d̪ukʰ], not [d̪ukkʰʌ]) nor lengthens a preceding vowel, which in पुनः and अतः also costs the final schwa the gold writes ([pun] for [punʌː]) — 6 of the 15 visarga words in the gold end in a length mark, only 2 of them outside a nasalised rhyme, so no single reading fits and none is guessed. Word-initial ⟨क्ष⟩ is likewise left at its cluster reading [ktsʰ] rather than the simplified [tsʰ] heard initially.",
