      "notes": "Guard ordered before NE_SCHWA_FINAL_*: the inherent schwa survives when the consonant carrying it is itself the second member of a conjunct, i.e. when the previous lattice slot is a bare (virama-bearing) consonant. This is the Nepali-versus-Hindi difference. Hindi deletes a word-final schwa after a cluster too (शब्द [ʃəbd̪]); Nepali does not tolerate the final cluster that would create, so समुद्र is [sʌmud̪rʌ], मित्र [mit̪rʌ], धर्म [d̪ʱʌrmʌ] and अर्थ [ʌrt̪ʰʌ] — 'Schwa is retained if the final syllable is a conjunct consonant' (Wikipedia, 'Nepali language', final-schwa retention rule 1). The identity rewrite fires first, and first-match-wins blocks the deletion rule behind it."
