    {
      "id": "NE_SCHWA_FINAL_b",
      "phonemes": [
        "bʌ"
      ],
      "surface": "b",
      "word_initial": false,
      "word_final": true,
      "notes": "Word-final schwa deletion. Nepali drops the inherent vowel of a word-final consonant letter that is not the second member of a conjunct: नाम [näm], वन [wʌn], नेपाल [nepäl]. Khatiwada (2009: 379) transcribes the standard-variety passage with no final schwa on such words (bʌtas 'wind', ek 'one'). Deletion is narrower than in Hindi in two ways: it is blocked after a conjunct (see NE_SCHWA_KEEP_b), and Nepali declares no medial VC_CV rule — the Hindi medial rule measurably over-deletes here, so किनभने stays [kinʌbʱʌne]. word_initial=false protects a one-letter monosyllable (न [nʌ]), whose schwa is the word's only vowel."
    },
